    {"also_covered_by": null,"also_ran_earlier": [],"band": "high","band_reason": null,"carried_by": 1,"desk": "ai","entities": ["openai"],"events": ["capex","earnings","funding"],"introduced_by_run": 1,"item_id": "ai-v7cet4gca37y4knv","lenses": ["markets"],"on_front_page": false,"published_at": "2026-11-06T22:53:46Z","rank_score": 1.601297,"reader_note": null,"same_story_as": null,"secondary_desk": null,"source_form": "article","source_id": "techcrunch-ai","source_kind": "reporting","source_name": "TechCrunch - AI","source_url": "https://techcrunch.com/2026/11/06/openai-reportedly-raises-an-additional-11-billion-at-a-1-25-trillion-valuation","summary": "Bloomberg reported that OpenAI raised $11 billion so far in a new funding round valuing the lab at $1.25 trillion, citing sources familiar with the matter on Friday. The round has not closed and could total about $20 billion. This falls short of an earlier $30 billion target. Bloomberg also reported that potential Anthropic buyers eye lower valuations amid market jitters.","time_source": "feed","title": "OpenAI reportedly raises $11 billion at $1.25 trillion valuation","truncated": false,"updated_at": null,"updated_by_run": null,"vertical": "ai","visual": null,"watchlist_hit": true},
    {"also_covered_by": null,"also_ran_earlier": [],"band": "high","band_reason": null,"carried_by": 1,"desk": null,"entities": ["amd","federal-reserve"],"events": [],"introduced_by_run": 1,"item_id": "ai-vxrvf7m480vacwh3","lenses": ["cyber","markets"],"on_front_page": false,"published_at": "2026-11-06T21:46:26Z","rank_score": 1.516288,"reader_note": null,"same_story_as": null,"secondary_desk": null,"source_form": "article","source_id": "straitstimes-business","source_kind": "reporting","source_name": "The Straits Times","source_url": "https://straitstimes.com/business/companies-markets/nasdaq-rebounds-but-posts-fourth-straight-weekly-decline","summary": "Technology stocks bounced back on Nov 6 from their worst drubbing in more than a year, yet the Nasdaq Composite posted a fourth consecutive weekly decline as investors weighed the AI trade. The index gained 1.09% to 24,579.43 points, while the Dow fell 0.46% to 51,286.21 points. Traders now price a 78% chance of a December Fed rate hike versus 70% earlier, as stocks teeter amid AI volatility and resilient payrolls showing an unemployment rate of 4.1%. Investors see the selloff into correction territory as a possible longer-term buying opportunity.","time_source": "feed","title": "Nasdaq rebounds but posts fourth straight weekly decline","truncated": false,"updated_at": null,"updated_by_run": null,"vertical": "ai","visual": null,"watchlist_hit": false},
    {"also_covered_by": null,"also_ran_earlier": [],"band": "high","band_reason": null,"carried_by": 1,"desk": null,"entities": [],"events": [],"introduced_by_run": 1,"item_id": "ai-hzj38943q73gcyfj","lenses": ["china","chips","cyber","markets"],"on_front_page": false,"published_at": "2026-11-06T21:00:00Z","rank_score": 1.499661,"reader_note": null,"same_story_as": null,"secondary_desk": null,"source_form": "article","source_id": "straitstimes-business","source_kind": "reporting","source_name": "The Straits Times","source_url": "https://straitstimes.com/business/ai-stock-rout-should-singapore-investors-buy-the-dip-or-cut-exposure","summary": "A sharp sell-off in US technology stocks spread across Asia and Singapore, prompting a debate over whether investors should buy the dip or cut exposure. The Nasdaq 100 plunged 6.4% on Nov 5, wiping out about US$2.5 trillion in value, while the S&P 500 fell 4.4% and the Philadelphia Semiconductor Index sank 12.2%. Saxo's Charu Chanana noted demand did not disappear overnight, while Invesco's Brian Levitt called it a reset of crowded trades. Advisors suggest long-term investors keep investing in strong companies, diversify or spread purchases over time, and those on margin assess how far prices could fall before needing more cash.","time_source": "feed","title": "Singapore investors weigh buying AI stocks after tech selloff","truncated": false,"updated_at": null,"updated_by_run": null,"vertical": "ai","visual": null,"watchlist_hit": false},
    {"also_covered_by": null,"also_ran_earlier": [],"band": "low","band_reason": "faithfulness","carried_by": 1,"desk": "world","entities": [],"events": [],"introduced_by_run": 1,"item_id": "ai-5r4g2gw75en2ytkx","lenses": ["china","trade"],"on_front_page": false,"published_at": "2026-11-06T21:00:00Z","rank_score": 1.499661,"reader_note": null,"same_story_as": null,"secondary_desk": "india","source_form": "article","source_id": "straitstimes-opinion","source_kind": "reporting","source_name": "The Straits Times","source_url": "https://straitstimes.com/opinion/will-the-world-ever-get-to-a-multipolar-order-four-propositions-to-consider","summary": "President Tharman Shanmugaratnam delivered a lecture at the Stockholm School of Economics on Oct 20, offering four propositions about global order. He argues that although US-led unipolarity has passed, a true multipolar order has not yet arrived: leadership has thinned and collective action to tackle climate change, pandemics and AI risks remains weak. Rather than fixate on the destination, he calls for bridge powers\u2014nations large and small\u2014to build coalitions and preserve economic networks between the US and China. He stresses that both superpowers must play integral roles in global governance, noting they together account for over 40% of greenhouse gas emissions. Citing a Rockefeller Foundation survey from 2026, he observes two-thirds of Americans favour global cooperation. His fourth proposition urges new social compacts at home to sustain an open international order.","time_source": "feed","title": "Tharman on whether a multipolar world is coming","truncated": false,"updated_at": null,"updated_by_run": null,"vertical": "ai","visual": null,"watchlist_hit": false},
    {"also_covered_by": null,"also_ran_earlier": [],"band": "high","band_reason": null,"carried_by": 1,"desk": null,"entities": [],"events": [],"introduced_by_run": 1,"item_id": "ai-mfedw043xgwz9mc4","lenses": ["trade"],"on_front_page": false,"published_at": "2026-11-06T22:13:00Z","rank_score": 1.476027,"reader_note": null,"same_story_as": null,"secondary_desk": null,"source_form": "article","source_id": "databricks-blog","source_kind": "announcement","source_name": "Databricks","source_url": "https://databricks.com/blog/read-dont-copy-replacing-file-feeds-opensharing-part-2-how-implement","summary": "This blog post, part two of a series on replacing file feeds with OpenSharing, walks through implementing recipient-side reads for a shared table. It focuses on the Databricks-to-Databricks case: an external recipient points a Structured Streaming reader at a provider's history-enabled Delta table and processes only new changes since its last checkpoint. An example shows two runs writing 200 rows instead of 1,200 because the checkpoint tracked versions read. The post notes that Change Data Feed is optional on Databricks Runtime 19 or above and explains how non-Spark recipients connect via a credentials file rather than metastore sharing IDs before recommending validating one table against one recipient.","time_source": "feed","title": "Implementing Databricks-to-Databricks sharing with OpenSharing tables","truncated": false,"updated_at": null,"updated_by_run": null,"vertical": "ai","visual": null,"watchlist_hit": false},
    {"also_covered_by": null,"also_ran_earlier": [],"band": "high","band_reason": null,"carried_by": 1,"desk": null,"entities": [],"events": [],"introduced_by_run": 1,"item_id": "ai-hhxdaxh81q6yj41e","lenses": [],"on_front_page": false,"published_at": "2026-11-06T22:10:00Z","rank_score": 1.474919,"reader_note": null,"same_story_as": null,"secondary_desk": null,"source_form": "article","source_id": "databricks-blog","source_kind": "announcement","source_name": "Databricks","source_url": "https://databricks.com/blog/read-dont-copy-replacing-file-and-api-feeds-opensharing-part-1-why","summary": "The article explains why teams should replace traditional customer data ingestion with OpenSharing, an open protocol for reading tables in place built on Delta Lake and Iceberg. It argues that existing patterns\u2014SFTP file dumps, API pulls, and managed connectors\u2014each carry hidden costs: pipeline management across per-customer quirks, timestamp drift from required time parameters, connection failures during backfills, duplicated storage, and no visibility into who accessed shared data. OpenSharing lets pipelines read a customer's table directly through cloud storage with their own compute, pull only changed records via a change data feed, resolve backfills by querying historical tables, avoid duplicate-storage costs, and provide an audit trail of every access using Databricks system tables\u2014replacing trust extended on faith with verifiable visibility.","time_source": "feed","title": "OpenSharing Replaces File and API Feeds","truncated": false,"updated_at": null,"updated_by_run": null,"vertical": "ai","visual": null,"watchlist_hit": false},
    {"also_covered_by": null,"also_ran_earlier": [],"band": "medium","band_reason": "hedge_dropped","carried_by": 1,"desk": null,"entities": ["tesla"],"events": [],"introduced_by_run": 1,"item_id": "energy-s7qn20apmvd8s3rf","lenses": [],"on_front_page": false,"published_at": "2026-11-06T20:44:43Z","rank_score": 1.554296,"reader_note": null,"same_story_as": null,"secondary_desk": null,"source_form": "article","source_id": "electrek","source_kind": "reporting","source_name": "Electrek","source_url": "https://electrek.co/2026/11/06/evgo-opens-its-first-tesla-superchargers-with-hundreds-more-planned","summary": "EVgo opened its first Supercharger station at Riverside Square & River's Edge, a grocery store parking lot on South Ashland Avenue in Chicago. The site has 12 fast-charging stalls with both NACS and CCS plugs plus Magic Dock technology so compatible vehicles charge without an adapter. EVgo owns the stations while Tesla runs them; hundreds more are planned across the US.","time_source": "feed","title": "EVgo Opens First Tesla Supercharger Station in Chicago","truncated": false,"updated_at": null,"updated_by_run": null,"vertical": "energy","visual": null,"watchlist_hit": true},
